<div class="meta">Drill 0020 · pairs with lesson 0020</div>
<h1>Drill: Socket Contracts</h1>

<div class="q"><h3>1. Adding a third head</h3>
<p>Under per-pair offsets vs socket contracts: how many entries does a new
"head_oval" require in each scheme, with 3 eye parts and 4 hair parts in
the catalog?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>Per-pair: 7 hand-tuned offsets (one per eye and hair part), each a
potential floating-eyeball bug. Socket contract: 1 PartDef with its own
<code>eyes</code> and <code>hair</code> sockets — every existing eye/hair
part snaps correctly with zero further edits.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>2. Scale order</h3>
<p>Why must the assembly resolve sockets BEFORE the character-height scale
is applied, rather than scaling each part to world size first?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>Sockets are authored in each part's local space. Scaling parts
individually would move their sockets relative to differently-scaled
neighbors — alignment drifts. Resolving in unscaled local space and then
applying ONE uniform scale to the finished figure preserves every relative
placement and satisfies the 1.8-unit contract in a single step.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>3. New category: hats</h3>
<p>List the exact data changes to add a Hat category that sits on the
head, without touching assembly code.</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>(1) Add <code>Hat</code> to PartCategory and bump
<code>kPartCategoryCount</code>; (2) one CategorySpec row
<code>{Hat, Head, "hat"}</code>; (3) add a <code>hat</code> socket to each
head PartDef; (4) hat parts (including a zero-size "hat_none"). The
recursive assembly, validator, randomizer, and picker all pick it up from
the spec table.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>4. Where's the one join between persona and look?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>The spawn path (main.cpp startup + onCreate): it loads both records by
character_id, builds the Npc from the persona, and registers the look for
rendering. Storage, editing, and validation never touch both — enforced by
two separate SQLite tables rather than convention.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>5. The exhaustive test's invariant</h3>
<p>The combination test asserts <code>assembled.ok == compatible</code>.
Why is the equality (not just implication) important?</p>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a">
<p>Both directions catch bugs: a compatible combo failing to assemble means
a missing socket (contract hole); an INcompatible combo assembling means
the style gate leaks and the picker could offer mixed-style characters.
The promise is "valid iff compatible", so the test asserts exactly that.</p>
</div></details></div>
